  "response": "The U.S. Census Bureau reports that women's median annual earnings for full-time, year-round work were about 84 percent of men's in 2022. The dispute over how to read that figure starts from two candidate sources, and at least one of them holds, perhaps both: men and women in the United States labor market are distributed unevenly across occupations and industries, and some employers pay lower wages to women than to equally productive men. The adjudication has proceeded by decomposition, by quasi-experiment, and by audit.\n\nBlau and Kahn's 2017 Journal of Economic Literature survey decomposed the gender wage gap using Oaxaca-Blinder techniques. The survey runs to some sixty pages, and in it Blau and Kahn concluded that measured differences in education and work experience fully account for the gender wage gap. The uneven distribution of men and women across occupations and industries produced one of that survey's central numbers: Blau and Kahn found that occupational and industry differences accounted for roughly half of the explained portion of the gender wage gap. Such a result cannot be squared with the proposition that measured differences in education and work experience fully account for the gender wage gap. Whereas the explained portion yields in this way to observable characteristics, the unexplained residual accounted for about 38 percent of the gender wage gap in 2010; in other words, roughly 38 percent of the 2010 gender pay differential remained unaccounted for by measured worker and job characteristics.\n\nClaudia Goldin's 2014 American Economic Association presidential address attributed the remaining gap largely to nonlinear returns to long and inflexible work hours. Bertrand, Goldin, and Katz found that the earnings gap among University of Chicago MBA graduates widened in the years following graduation, a widening that shows career interruptions and shorter weekly hours to be the principal drivers of that gap. The mechanism thereby identified, namely the penalties attached to time away from work and to reduced weekly hours, confirms Goldin's reading of the residual.\n\nChronologically, the Danish evidence came afterward; only in the years following that presidential address did the child-penalty literature acquire its canonical form. Child penalty estimates in Denmark were produced with event-study designs centered on the birth of a first child. Kleven, Landais, and S\u00f8gaard worked with population registers spanning several decades, and they found that Danish mothers' earnings fully recover within three years of childbirth. By contrast, the laboratory experiment by Correll, Benard, and Paik showed that mothers received fewer callbacks than equally qualified childless women.\n\nThe settlement reached in this literature is therefore a division of explanatory labor rather than a verdict for one camp. Decomposition fixes how much of the differential measured characteristics absorb, models of hours and time away from work interpret a substantial part of what remains, and experimental evidence locates employer treatment of women with children at precisely the point where productivity is held constant by design.",
